### # 2. A Blood Reservoir in the Brain (Anatomical Metaphor)
<a id="term-2-a-blood-reservoir-in-the-brain-anatomical-metaphor"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-blood-reservoir-in-the-brain-anatomical-metaphor)

Meaning: The most frequent use of the word in these specific medical texts is anatomical.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-blood-reservoir-in-the-brain-anatomical-metaphor-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

The most frequent use of the word in these specific medical texts is anatomical.

##### Galen, *De usu partium* (Kühn vol. 3, p. 708) *(Note: This exact anatomical description is also repeated in Galen's De anatomicis administrationibus and Oribasius's Collectiones medicae).*
<a id="cit-2-a-blood-reservoir-in-the-brain-anatomical-metaphor-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> "...συμβάλλουσι δὲ κατὰ τὴν κορυφὴν τῆς κεφαλῆς αἱ παράγουσαι τὸ αἷμα διπλώσεις τῆς μήνιγγος εἰς χώραν τινὰ κοινὴν οἷον δεξαμενήν, ἣν δὴ καὶ δι' αὐτὸ τοῦτο προσαγορεύειν ἔθος ἐστὶν Ἡροφίλῳ ληνόν..."

**Translation**

> "...and the folds of the meninx that channel the blood converge at the top of the head into a certain common space, like a cistern, which for this very reason it is the custom of Herophilus to call a vat (*lenos*)..."

Reference: Galen, *De usu partium* (Kühn vol. 3, p. 708) *(Note: This exact anatomical description is also repeated in Galen's De anatomicis administrationibus and Oribasius's Collectiones medicae).*

Context note: The most frequent use of the word in these specific medical texts is anatomical.

### # 2. A Case or Casket for Perfume Flasks
<a id="term-2-a-case-or-casket-for-perfume-flasks"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-case-or-casket-for-perfume-flasks)

Meaning: In another group of glosses, *myrothēkē* denotes not the flask itself but the case in which alabaster perfume vessels are kept.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-case-or-casket-for-perfume-flasks-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In another group of glosses, *myrothēkē* denotes not the flask itself but the case in which alabaster perfume vessels are kept.

##### Harpocration, *Lexicon in decem oratores Atticos* (p. 20, l. 6)
<a id="cit-2-a-case-or-casket-for-perfume-flasks-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `supporting_lexical`
Usage type: `lexicon`

**Greek**

> `Ἀλαβαστοθῆκαι: αἱ θῆκαι τῶν ἀλαβάστων, ἃς ἐν τῇ συνηθείᾳ μυροθήκας καλοῦσι· ἀλάβαστοι δ' εἰσὶ λήκυθοι ὧν οὐκ ἔστι λαβέσθαι διὰ λειότητα.`

**Translation**

> “*Alabastothēkai*: the cases for alabaster flasks, which in ordinary usage they call perfume-cases; alabasters are flasks that cannot be grasped because of their smoothness.”

Reference: Harpocration, *Lexicon in decem oratores Atticos* (p. 20, l. 6)

Context note: In another group of glosses, *myrothēkē* denotes not the flask itself but the case in which alabaster perfume vessels are kept.

### # 2. A Mixing Tub for Spices and Liquids
<a id="term-2-a-mixing-tub-for-spices-and-liquids"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-mixing-tub-for-spices-and-liquids)

Meaning: Beyond just soaking fresh flowers, the *louter* is also used as a mixing tub where dry aromatic spices (like cinnamon and saffron) and water are combined with previously pressed oils.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-mixing-tub-for-spices-and-liquids-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

Beyond just soaking fresh flowers, the *louter* is also used as a mixing tub where dry aromatic spices (like cinnamon and saffron) and water are combined with previously pressed oils.

##### Dioscorides, *De materia medica* (Book 1, chapter 52, section 4)
<a id="cit-2-a-mixing-tub-for-spices-and-liquids-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `recipe`

**Greek**

> "...κρόκου καὶ κιναμώμου τὸ ἴσον κόψας σήσας ἔμβαλε εἰς τὸν λουτῆρα μεθ' ὕδατος καὶ ἐπίχει τὸ πρῶτον ἐκπιεσθὲν μύρον..."

**Translation**

> "...having pounded and sifted an equal amount of saffron and cinnamon, put them into the basin (*louter*) with water, and pour on the first-pressed ointment..."

Reference: Dioscorides, *De materia medica* (Book 1, chapter 52, section 4)

Context note: Beyond just soaking fresh flowers, the *louter* is also used as a mixing tub where dry aromatic spices (like cinnamon and saffron) and water are combined with previously pressed oils.

### # 2. A perfume-shop as a commercial establishment or business asset
<a id="term-2-a-perfume-shop-as-a-commercial-establishment-or-business-asset"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-perfume-shop-as-a-commercial-establishment-or-business-asset)

Meaning: In legal and financial contexts, *myropōlion* can denote the perfumery establishment as something transferred, purchased, or burdened with debts.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-perfume-shop-as-a-commercial-establishment-or-business-asset-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In legal and financial contexts, *myropōlion* can denote the perfumery establishment as something transferred, purchased, or burdened with debts.

##### Hyperides, *In Athenogenem* (col. 4)
<a id="cit-2-a-perfume-shop-as-a-commercial-establishment-or-business-asset-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `appendix_context`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> `ἐλθόντες δ' ἐπὶ τὸ μυροπώλιον τὸ μὲν γραμματεῖον τιθέμεθα παρὰ Λυσικλεῖ Λευκονοεῖ· τὰς δὲ τετταράκοντα μνᾶς ἐγὼ καταβαλὼν τὴν ὠνὴν ἐποιησάμην`

**Translation**

> “When we came to the perfume-shop, we deposited the document with Lysikles of Leukonoe, and I paid the forty minas and completed the purchase.”

Reference: Hyperides, *In Athenogenem* (col. 4)

Context note: In legal and financial contexts, *myropōlion* can denote the perfumery establishment as something transferred, purchased, or burdened with debts.

### # 2. A perfumer as a shop-based artisan
<a id="term-2-a-perfumer-as-a-shop-based-artisan"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-perfumer-as-a-shop-based-artisan)

Meaning: The word can also denote the perfumer as one among several artisans and shopkeepers in the urban marketplace.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-perfumer-as-a-shop-based-artisan-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

The word can also denote the perfumer as one among several artisans and shopkeepers in the urban marketplace.

##### Joannes Chrysostomus, *Contra eos qui subintroductas habent virgines* 10.26
<a id="cit-2-a-perfumer-as-a-shop-based-artisan-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `appendix_context`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> `εἶτα ἀπὸ τοῦ μυρεψοῦ πρὸς τὸν τὰς ὀθόνας πωλοῦντα`

**Translation**

> “Then from the perfumer to the man who sells linens.”

Reference: Joannes Chrysostomus, *Contra eos qui subintroductas habent virgines* 10.26

Context note: The word can also denote the perfumer as one among several artisans and shopkeepers in the urban marketplace.

### # 2. A Porous Barrier / Lid for Vapor Infusion
<a id="term-2-a-porous-barrier-lid-for-vapor-infusion"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-porous-barrier-lid-for-vapor-infusion)

Meaning: During the preparation of quince oil (*melinon*), a loosely woven rush mat (*psiathos araia*) is placed across the mouth of a wide vessel containing hot oil.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-porous-barrier-lid-for-vapor-infusion-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

During the preparation of quince oil (*melinon*), a loosely woven rush mat (*psiathos araia*) is placed across the mouth of a wide vessel containing hot oil.

##### Dioscorides, *De materia medica* (Book 1, chapter 45, section 1)
<a id="cit-2-a-porous-barrier-lid-for-vapor-infusion-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `recipe`

**Greek**

> "...εἶτα ἀπηθήσας τὸ ἔλαιον βάλε εἰς ἀγγεῖον πλατύστομον, ἐπιθείς τε ῥῖπον ἐκ καλάμου ἢ ψίαθον ἀραιὰν ἄνωθέν τε ἐπιθεὶς κυδώνια μῆλα καὶ περικαλύψας ἱματίοις ἔασον ἡμέρας ἱκανάς..."

**Translation**

> "...then having strained the oil, put it into a wide-mouthed vessel, and having placed upon it a hurdle of reeds or a loosely-woven rush mat (*psiathon araian*), and having placed quinces on top and covered them with cloths, leave them for a sufficient number of days..."

Reference: Dioscorides, *De materia medica* (Book 1, chapter 45, section 1)

Context note: During the preparation of quince oil (*melinon*), a loosely woven rush mat (*psiathos araia*) is placed across the mouth of a wide vessel containing hot oil.

### # 2. A professional handler and keeper of perfume stock
<a id="term-2-a-professional-handler-and-keeper-of-perfume-stock"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-professional-handler-and-keeper-of-perfume-stock)

Meaning: In technical discussion, the *myropōlēs* is not just a shopkeeper but someone who knows how perfumes must be preserved.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-professional-handler-and-keeper-of-perfume-stock-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In technical discussion, the *myropōlēs* is not just a shopkeeper but someone who knows how perfumes must be preserved.

##### Theophrastus, *Fragmenta* 4.40
<a id="cit-2-a-professional-handler-and-keeper-of-perfume-stock-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `appendix_context`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> `διὸ καὶ οἱ μυροπῶλαι ζητοῦσι τὰς οἰκίας ὑπερῴους καὶ μὴ προσηλίους ἀλλ' ὅτι μάλιστα παλισκίους`

**Translation**

> “That is why perfume-sellers look for upper rooms that are not sunny, but as shaded as possible.”

Reference: Theophrastus, *Fragmenta* 4.40

Context note: In technical discussion, the *myropōlēs* is not just a shopkeeper but someone who knows how perfumes must be preserved.

### # 2. A Receptacle for Counterweights (Orthopedics)
<a id="term-2-a-receptacle-for-counterweights-orthopedics"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-2-a-receptacle-for-counterweights-orthopedics)

Meaning: In the Hippocratic treatise *De articulis* (On Joints) and Apollonius of Kition's commentary on it, the *sphyris* is used as an improvised mechanical tool for setting dislocated hips.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-2-a-receptacle-for-counterweights-orthopedics-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In the Hippocratic treatise *De articulis* (On Joints) and Apollonius of Kition's commentary on it, the *sphyris* is used as an improvised mechanical tool for setting dislocated hips.

##### Hippocrates, *De articulis* (Section 78) *(Repeated almost verbatim by Apollonius of Kition in his commentary).*
<a id="cit-2-a-receptacle-for-counterweights-orthopedics-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `appendix_context`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> "...ἐκ δὲ τοῦ σιναροῦ ἐς κεράμιον ὕδωρ ἐγχέαντα ἐκκρεμάσαι ἢ ἐς σφυρίδα λίθους ἐμβαλόντα."

**Translation**

> "...and from the injured [leg], having poured water into an earthen pot, hang it, or having cast stones into a basket (*sphyrida*) [hang it]."

Reference: Hippocrates, *De articulis* (Section 78) *(Repeated almost verbatim by Apollonius of Kition in his commentary).*

Context note: In the Hippocratic treatise *De articulis* (On Joints) and Apollonius of Kition's commentary on it, the *sphyris* is used as an improvised mechanical tool for setting dislocated hips.

