#### Default sense
<a id="sense-3-by-metaphor-a-repository-of-varied-and-useful-material-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

Later prose extends the concrete image of the perfume-shop into a metaphor for a place where many different fine or useful things are gathered together.

##### Constantinus VII Porphyrogenitus, *De sententiis* (p. 72)
<a id="cit-3-by-metaphor-a-repository-of-varied-and-useful-material-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `appendix_context`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> `καὶ περιφέρει γε τὴν ἱστορίαν ἐκ πολλῶν καὶ παντοδαπῶν τῶν ταῦτα εἰρηκότων ὥσπερ ῥῶπόν τινα ποικίλον καὶ χρήσιμον εἰς ἓν μυροπώλιον τὴν ἰδίαν ἐξήγησιν κατακεκλειμένην καὶ συνηγμένην`

**Translation**

> “And he brings together his history from many and varied sources, as though he had packed assorted and useful wares into a single perfume-shop within his own exposition.”

Reference: Constantinus VII Porphyrogenitus, *De sententiis* (p. 72)

Context note: Later prose extends the concrete image of the perfume-shop into a metaphor for a place where many different fine or useful things are gathered together.

### # 3. Galen on moving from a ceramic vessel to a double perfumer’s vessel, then to another perfumer’s vessel, and finally to glass or silver
<a id="term-3-galen-on-moving-from-a-ceramic-vessel-to-a-double-perfumer-s-vessel-then-to-another-perfumer-s-vessel-and-finally-to-glass-or-silver"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-3-galen-on-moving-from-a-ceramic-vessel-to-a-double-perfumer-s-vessel-then-to-another-perfumer-s-vessel-and-finally-to-glass-or-silver)

Meaning: **Greek** > ...

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-3-galen-on-moving-from-a-ceramic-vessel-to-a-double-perfumer-s-vessel-then-to-another-perfumer-s-vessel-and-finally-to-glass-or-silver-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

**Greek** > ...

##### ** Galenus, *De compositione medicamentorum secundum locos* x, Kühn 13.53.14 ff.
<a id="cit-3-galen-on-moving-from-a-ceramic-vessel-to-a-double-perfumer-s-vessel-then-to-another-perfumer-s-vessel-and-finally-to-glass-or-silver-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> ... καὶ βάλλοντες εἰς κεραμεοῦν ἀγγεῖον, ἐπιβάλλομεν αὐταῖς ὕδατος γλυκέος ... καὶ θέντες ἐπὶ πυρὸς ἕψομεν. ὅταν δὲ εἰς ἥμισυ καταχθῇ, καθαιροῦμεν καὶ διϋλίζομεν εἰς διπλοῦν ἀγγεῖον μυρεψικόν. καὶ τὰς μὲν ῥίζας ἐκθλίψαντες ῥίπτομεν, εἰς δὲ τὸ ὕδωρ ἐπιβάλλομεν ἁδρομερῶς κεκομμένα ... εἶτ' ἐπὶ τὸ πῦρ ἐπιθέντες πάλιν ἕψομεν, καὶ ὅταν εἰς ἡμίσειαν καταντήσῃ, πάλιν διϋλίζομεν εἰς ἕτερον ἀγγεῖον μυρεψικὸν μικρόν. ... μεταίρομεν εἰς ἀγγεῖον ὑελοῦν ἢ ἀργυροῦν.

**Translation**

> ... putting them into a ceramic vessel, we pour sweet water over them ... and setting it on the fire we boil it. When it has been reduced to half, we skim it and strain it into a double perfumer’s vessel. After squeezing out the roots, we throw them away, and into the liquid we put the other ingredients, cut in coarse pieces ... then setting it on the fire again we boil it once more, and when it has come down to half, we again strain it into another small perfumer’s vessel. ... we transfer it into a glass or silver vessel.

Reference: ** Galenus, *De compositione medicamentorum secundum locos* x, Kühn 13.53.14 ff.

Context note: **Greek** > ...

### # 3. *Louteridion*: A Small Basin for Re-Maceration (Diminutive)
<a id="term-3-louteridion-a-small-basin-for-re-maceration-diminutive"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-3-louteridion-a-small-basin-for-re-maceration-diminutive)

Meaning: The text also uses the diminutive form, *louteridion* (a small basin or small tub).

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-3-louteridion-a-small-basin-for-re-maceration-diminutive-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

The text also uses the diminutive form, *louteridion* (a small basin or small tub).

##### Dioscorides, *De materia medica* (Book 1, chapter 43, section 1)
<a id="cit-3-louteridion-a-small-basin-for-re-maceration-diminutive-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `recipe`

**Greek**

> "...βαλὼν δὲ εἰς λουτηρίδιον τὰ ἐξιπωθέντα ῥόδα ἐπίχει λίτρας ὀκτὼ οὐγγίας τρεῖς τοῦ ἐστυμμένου ἐλαίου καὶ πάλιν ἐξίπου."

**Translation**

> "...and having put the pressed-out roses into a small basin (*louteridion*), pour on eight litrai and three ounces of the thickened oil and press it out again."

Reference: Dioscorides, *De materia medica* (Book 1, chapter 43, section 1)

Context note: The text also uses the diminutive form, *louteridion* (a small basin or small tub).

