### # Outer water-vessel for indirect heating
<a id="term-outer-water-vessel-for-indirect-heating"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-outer-water-vessel-for-indirect-heating)

Meaning: In compound pharmacology, **κακκάβη** can name the outer pot that holds hot water while a second container is set inside it.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-outer-water-vessel-for-indirect-heating-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In compound pharmacology, **κακκάβη** can name the outer pot that holds hot water while a second container is set inside it.

##### Galen, *De compositione medicamentorum per genera*, 7.13 (Kühn 13.629)
<a id="cit-outer-water-vessel-for-indirect-heating-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> ὀνομάζομεν δὲ οὕτως, ὅταν ἐν κακκάβῃ θερμὸν ὕδωρ ἐχούσῃ σκεῦος ἕτερον ἐνίσταται μετὰ τῶν τηκτῶν ἔχον καὶ τὴν χαλβάνην, ὑποκαιομένης τῆς κακκάβης.

**Translation**

> “We call it this when another vessel containing the meltable substances and galbanum is set inside a *kakkábē* holding hot water, while the *kakkábē* is heated from below.”

Reference: Galen, *De compositione medicamentorum per genera*, 7.13 (Kühn 13.629)

Context note: In compound pharmacology, **κακκάβη** can name the outer pot that holds hot water while a second container is set inside it.

### # Perfumery vessel for scented oils
<a id="term-perfumery-vessel-for-scented-oils"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-perfumery-vessel-for-scented-oils)

Meaning: In perfumery recipes a βῖκος is the jar into which oil and aromatic flowers are put for infusion.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-perfumery-vessel-for-scented-oils-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In perfumery recipes a βῖκος is the jar into which oil and aromatic flowers are put for infusion.

##### Paulus, *Epitomae medicae libri septem*, 7.20.4
<a id="cit-perfumery-vessel-for-scented-oils-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> Ῥόδων ἐρυθρῶν ὠνυχισμένων καὶ διεψυγμένων νυχθήμερον <α> 𐆄 <γ>, ἐλαίου ὀμφακίνου ξ̸ Ἰταλικὸν <α>· περισφίγξας ἐπιμελῶς τὸν βῖκον, ὡς μὴ διαπνεῖσθαι, ἡλίου ἐν ὑπαίθρῳ ἐπὶ <μ> ἡμέρας

**Translation**

> With three ounces of red roses, trimmed and cooled for a day and a night, and one Italian sextarius of omphacine oil, after carefully binding the jar so that it does not vent, keep it in the open air under the sun for forty days.

Reference: Paulus, *Epitomae medicae libri septem*, 7.20.4

Context note: In perfumery recipes a βῖκος is the jar into which oil and aromatic flowers are put for infusion.

### # Pharmacological and Therapeutic Implements
<a id="term-pharmacological-and-therapeutic-implements"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-pharmacological-and-therapeutic-implements)

Meaning: In Late Antique medical treatises, parts of the *lekythos* are integrated directly into pharmaceutical recipes and physical therapies.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-pharmacological-and-therapeutic-implements-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In Late Antique medical treatises, parts of the *lekythos* are integrated directly into pharmaceutical recipes and physical therapies.

##### Pseudo-Galenus Med., *De remediis parabilibus libri iii*, Volume 14, page 483, line 10 *(Identical instruction also found in Oribasius Med., Libri ad Eunapium 4.115.6)*
<a id="cit-pharmacological-and-therapeutic-implements-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `unknown`

**Greek**

> Λαβὼν βύσμα ἀπὸ ληκύθου βίβλινον περιείλησον ὀθονίῳ, ἔπειτα μάνναν ὠοῦ τῷ λευκῷ ἀναλαβὼν κατάχρισον καὶ ἔνθες εἰς τὸν ὀμφαλὸν καὶ ἐπίδει προστύπως.

**Translation**

> "Taking a papyrus stopper from an oil-flask, wrap it in a linen cloth, then taking incense dust mixed with egg white, smear it on, place it into the navel, and bind it so that it forms a mold."

Reference: Pseudo-Galenus Med., *De remediis parabilibus libri iii*, Volume 14, page 483, line 10 *(Identical instruction also found in Oribasius Med., Libri ad Eunapium 4.115.6)*

Context note: In Late Antique medical treatises, parts of the *lekythos* are integrated directly into pharmaceutical recipes and physical therapies.

### # Pharmacological glass jar
<a id="term-pharmacological-glass-jar"></a>

Overview: [back to tools.md](./tools.md#term-pharmacological-glass-jar)

Meaning: In medical recipes βῖκος denotes a glass vessel used to receive and preserve medicinal substances.

#### Default sense
<a id="sense-pharmacological-glass-jar-sense-1"></a>

Relevance: `non_perfume`

In medical recipes βῖκος denotes a glass vessel used to receive and preserve medicinal substances.

##### Dioscorides, *De materia medica*, 2.78.1
<a id="cit-pharmacological-glass-jar-cit-1"></a>

Source class: `primary_technical`
Usage type: `recipe`

**Greek**

> τὰς δὲ εἰς τὰ ὀφθαλμικὰ λίνῳ ἀποδήσας βάλε εἰς βῖκον ὑελοῦν ἔχοντα μέλι, καὶ τὴν ἀρχὴν τοῦ λίνου περιδήσας τῷ στόματι τοῦ βίκου πωμάσας ἀπόθου

**Translation**

> For the portions meant for eye-medicines, after tying them with linen, put them into a glass jar containing honey; then, after fastening the end of the linen to the mouth of the jar and stoppering it, store it.

Reference: Dioscorides, *De materia medica*, 2.78.1

Context note: In medical recipes βῖκος denotes a glass vessel used to receive and preserve medicinal substances.

